{"abstract":"In 1919 Einsteins theory of general relativity was verified for the first time by the Eddington-experiment. During a total solar eclipse, it was possible to measure the position of stars near the Sun and then compare these apparent positions to those of the stars at night months earlier. The results showed a gravitational deflection of about 1.75'' as light from distant stars passed near the Sun. Since then, a heap of new technology has been developed and improved on, making it possible for even amateur astronomers to carry out their own Eddington-experiment. In this poster I have examined digital images taken during the 2017 solar eclipse (as observed from the U.S.) and analysed the observations using the software package 'MEE2024 Stacker v0.4.5' by Andrew and Douglas Smith. Considering atmospheric diffraction and optical distortion it was possible to measure the predicted deflection angle of 1.75'' within an error under 10%.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Pedersen, Johanne Hein","Hinse, Tobias Cornelius"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-821318"],"event":{"end":"2025-05-21","place":"Vejle, dk","start":"2025-05-20","title":"Annual Danish Astronomy Meeting 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Pedersen, Johanne Hein"],"keyword":["Data analysis","GR","General relativity","solar eclipse"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the modern eddington experiment testing einstein s general theory of relativity from observing a total solar eclipse","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15534207","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work received financial support from the Instrument Center for Danish Astrophysics (IDA)"],"id":"S01-000.00-821318","ids":["sdu-6a2fbd7d-c381-4f25-aaaa-5535c2d9975a"],"keyword":["GR","MEE","data analysis","eddington experiment","general relativity","solar eclipse"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-542394AB53CEA48D13E66159FA4D518C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15534207","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Physics, Chemistry and Pharmacy","SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Physics, Chemistry and Pharmacy, Physics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Johanne Hein","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"3677e48e-6f95-423b-9958-e3c0a2e9c739","name":"Pedersen, Johanne Hein","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tobias Cornelius","key":"hinseoth","last":"Hinse","locid":"f7a3a4fc-432b-4f10-9791-f1498d07e261","name":"Hinse, Tobias Cornelius","orcid":"0000-0001-8870-3146","rank":2,"role":"Other","rolecode":"oth"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-6a2fbd7d-c381-4f25-aaaa-5535c2d9975a"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The Modern Eddington Experiment : Testing Einstein's General Theory of Relativity from Observing a Total Solar Eclipse","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-821318","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"To investigate levels of specific plasma-biomarkers related to neurodegeneration and inflammation in patients with different chronic degenerative retinal diseases, using an ultrasensitive technology called ‘single molecule array’ (SiMoA). Also, to investigate if biomarkers were measurable in the patient’s blood, dependent on age and medical comorbidities, and useful for stratifying the diseases. This exploratory, cross-sectional study recruited 151 adults at the Department of Ophthalmology, Rigshospitalet, Denmark (period 2019 to 2020). Clinical data came from the electronic medical-record system. The study population consisted of 131 patients: 32 with diabetic retinopathy (DR; 51 diabetes, DM), 27 with glaucoma, 53 with inherited retinal degeneration (IRD and 20 healthy controls (HC). Medical comorbidities included organ failure, other active eye diseases, and comorbidities. Three biomarkers, neurofilament-light-chain (NFL), glial-fibrillary-acidic-protein (GFAP), and CXC-motif chemokine ligand 13 (CXCL13), were measured with SiMoA technology. The age-adjusted values were reported as fold differences (FD) with 95% confidence intervals (CI). Increased NFL levels were found in DR patients compared to HCs (FD 1.81 95%CI 1.43, 2.28, p < 0.001, adj-p < 0.001). Similarly increased NFL levels were reported in advanced DR (PDR, DME), compared to both DM (FD 2.52 (95%CI: 1.71; 3.72, p < 0.001, adj-p < 0.001, and FD 2.04 (95%CI: 1.33; 3.12, p < 0.001, adj-p < 0.001), respectively) and HCs (FD 2.35 (95%CI: 1.67; 3.30, p < 0.001, adj-p < 0.001), and FD 1.89 (95%CI: 1.28; 2.79, p < 0.001, adj-p < 0.001) respectively). Independent of comorbidities, decreased NFL-levels were seen in IRD compared to DR (FD 0.49 (95% CI 0.39; 0.61, p < 0.001; adj-p < 0.001), ±comorbidities). Decreased GFAP levels were seen in DM patients compared to HCs (FD 0.69; 95%CI 0.55, 0.87, p = 0.002, adj-p = 0.02), but contrary to an increasing trend in advanced DR compared to DM (-comorbidities). These results imply that these biomarker-tests are useful for detecting and monitoring development of retinopathy in the circulations of diabetes patients. Plasma-biomarkers may be useful to stratify between retinal disease types. Prospective studies are underway to explore this hypothesis in depth.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Hajari, Javad Nouri","Ilginis, Tomas","Pedersen, Tobias Torp","Lønkvist, Claes Sepstrup","Saunte, Jon Peiter","Hofsli, Mikael","Schmidt, Diana Chabane","Al-abaiji, Hajer Ahmad","Ahmed, Yasmeen","Bach-Holm, Daniella","Kessel, Line","Kolko, Miriam","Bertelsen, Mette","Larsen, Lars Michael","Sørensen, Frederik","Forman, Julie Lyng","Olsen, Dorte Aalund","Rosenberg, Thomas","Brandslund, Ivan","Slidsborg, Carina"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-804487"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Slidsborg, Carina"],"keyword":["Age","Confounders","Diagnosis","Disease monitoring","Plasma biomarkers","chronic retinal degenerative disease","diabetic microvascular complication","medical comorbidities"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"novel blood biomarkers to detect retinal neurodegeneration and inflammation in diabetic retinopathy","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms26062625","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-804487","identifier":[{"id":"40141267","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105001105678","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11942211","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-69c01bc2-082c-4292-882e-00cd19b406d7","sdu-2b410af8-01e0-4c09-bc02-32dc94474be9"],"keyword":["age