,"rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mikkel","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"d57baeeb-925d-48c8-84c0-f23fbbfb0db5","name":"Sørensen, Mikkel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tobias","key":"richterpau","last":"Richter","locid":"eacd6ca8-b280-46dd-bd5c-7405e386b59b","name":"Richter, Tobias","orcid":"0000-0001-9902-8852","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Patrick","key":"pedersenpedh","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Patrick","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Anne","key":"jörgensenlindahlpedh","last":"Jörgensen-Lindahl","name":"Jörgensen-Lindahl, Anne","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Mikkel","key":"sørensenpedh","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Mikkel","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Tobias","key":"richterpedh","last":"Richter","name":"Richter, Tobias","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2307/j.ctv2b07trt.3","isbn":["9781789694789"],"publisher":"Archaeopress Publishing Ltd.","series":"Proceedings of the 3rd Meeting of the Association of Ground Stone Tools Research","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 3rd Meeting of the Association of Ground Stone Tools Research"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-1400e73d-337e-4b35-8388-e2314ae50fd6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Introduction: Ground Stone Tools and Past Foodways 3rd Meeting of the Association for Ground Stone Research","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-405365","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Anti-programmed death (PD)-1 (aPD1) therapy is an effective treatment for metastatic melanoma (MM); however, over 50% of patients progress due to resistance. We tested a first-in-class immune-modulatory vaccine (IO102/IO103) against indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase (IDO) and PD ligand 1 (PD-L1), targeting immunosuppressive cells and tumor cells expressing IDO and/or PD-L1 (IDO/PD-L1), combined with nivolumab. Thirty aPD1 therapy-naive patients with MM were treated in a phase 1/2 study (https://clinicaltrials.gov/, NCT03047928). The primary endpoint was feasibility and safety; the systemic toxicity profile was comparable to that of nivolumab monotherapy. Secondary endpoints were efficacy and immunogenicity; an objective response rate (ORR) of 80% (confidence interval (CI), 62.7–90.5%) was reached, with 43% (CI, 27.4–60.8%) complete responses. After a median follow-up of 22.9 months, the median progression-free survival (PFS) was 26 months (CI, 15.4–69 months). Median overall survival (OS) was not reached. Vaccine-specific responses assessed in vitro were detected in the blood of >93% of patients during vaccination. Vaccine-reactive T cells comprised CD4+ and CD8+ T cells with activity against IDO- and PD-L1-expressing cancer and immune cells. T cell influx of peripherally expanded T cells into tumor sites was observed in responding patients, and general enrichment of IDO- and PD-L1-specific clones after treatment was documented. These clinical efficacy and favorable safety data support further validation in a larger randomized trial to confirm the clinical potential of this immunomodulating approach.","author_count":17,"contributors":["Kjeldsen, Julie Westerlin","Lorentzen, Cathrine Lund","Martinenaite, Evelina","Ellebaek, Eva","Donia, Marco","Holmstroem, Rikke Boedker","Klausen, Tobias Wirenfeldt","Madsen, Cecilie Oelvang","Ahmed, Shamaila Munir","Weis-Banke, Stine Emilie","Holmström, Morten Orebo","Hendel, Helle Westergren","Ehrnrooth, Eva","Zocca, Mai Britt","Pedersen, Ayako Wakatsuki","Andersen, Mads Hald","Svane, Inge Marie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-538188"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a phase 000000000001 000000000002 trial of an immune modulatory vaccine against ido pd l000000000001 in combination with nivolumab in metastatic melanoma","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-021-01544-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-538188","identifier":[{"id":"34887574","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85120935004","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8904254","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-f2858f13-f435-44f9-ad03-8b2305544579"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-537BA70E5B2EA610C29931A35F12E84E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/314958988/s41591_021_01544_x.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-021-01544-x","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","countr