{"abstract":"Nanozymes, nanoparticles that mimic the natural activity of enzymes, are intriguing academically and are important in the context of the Origin of Life. However, current nanozymes offer mimicry of a narrow range of mammalian enzymes, near-exclusively performing redox reactions. We present an unexpected discovery of non-proteinaceous enzymes based on metals, metal oxides, 1D/2D-materials, and non-metallic nanomaterials. The specific novelty of these findings lies in the identification of nanozymes with apparent mimicry of diverse mammalian enzymes, including unique pan-glycosidases. Further novelty lies in the identification of the substrate scope for the lead candidates, specifically in the context of bioconversion of glucuronides, that is, human metabolites and privileged prodrugs in the field of enzyme-prodrug therapies. Lastly, nanozymes are employed for conversion of glucuronide prodrugs into marketed anti-inflammatory and antibacterial agents, as well as “nanozyme prodrug therapy” to mediate antibacterial measures.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Walther, Raoul","Winther, Anna","Fruergaard, Anne Sofie","Akker, Wouter van den","Sørensen, Lise","Nielsen, Signe Maria","Olesen, Morten Tobias Jarlstad","Dai, Yitao","Jeppesen, Henrik S.","Lamagni, Paolo","Savateev, Aleksandra","Pedersen, Søren L.","Riber, Camilla Frich","Vigier, Cécile Aude","Lock, Nina","Singh, Mandeep","Bansal, Vipul","Meyer, Rikke Louise","Zelikin, Alexander N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-148310"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Max Planck Institute of Colloids and Interfaces","Royal Melbourne Institute of Technology University"],"keyword":["Enzyme prodrug therapy","Glucuronide","Nanozymes","Prodrugs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"identification and directed development of non organic catalysts with apparent pan enzymatic mimicry into nanozymes for efficient prodrug conversion","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85057456567","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-148310","identifier":[{"id":"85057456567","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30408323","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-212299d1-9b09-42bd-8c9b-aa2f9fc85ae3"],"keyword":["enzyme mimicry","enzyme-prodrug therapy","glucuronide","nanozymes","prodrugs"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6780564E8C5E327E636503689A8D2435"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"max planck institute of colloids and interfaces","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Max Planck Institute of Colloids and Interfaces","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"royal melbourne institute of technology university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Royal Melbourne Institute of Technology University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Raoul","key":"waltherpau","last":"Walther","locid":"994eba41-cfe2-44bb-9280-b648f7292d72","name":"Walther, Raoul","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"wintherpau","last":"Winther","locid":"723ed100-b0c9-4c12-9a0b-0bbbb1fe2501","name":"Winther