{"abstract":"This review presents the consequences of gallstones lost during laparoscopic cholecystectomy (LC) and left in the peritoneal cavity. The incidence of intraoperative loss of gallstones during LC is estimated to be 1-40%. 1-12% of patients with unretrieved gallstones may develop compli-cations, with intra-abdominal abscesses being the most frequent. The presence of pigment stones is correlated to development of complications. Studies show, that 80-83% of surgeons may underestimate the number of potential complications, which indicates, that this subject requires increased clinical attention.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Ross Clemmesen, Tobias Bastian","Achiam, Michael Patrick","Pedersen, Christian Ross"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-422206"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"svære komplikationer efter tab af galdesten under laparoskopisk kolecystektomi","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://ugeskriftet.dk/videnskab/svaere-komplikationer-efter-tab-af-galdesten-under-laparoskopisk-kolecystektomi","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-422206","identifier":[{"id":"85074228024","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30020070","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-303d2a92-45c6-4061-9fb0-fae9274cc04c"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-5EFC278A22ADFB39E1C4AEFC5F228E1F"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Tobias Bastian","key":"rossclemmesenpau","last":"Ross Clemmesen","name":"Ross Clemmesen, Tobias Bastian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael Patrick","key":"achiampau","last":"Achiam","locid":"dfd48120-815f-4309-a218-ca71a476dfda","name":"Achiam, Michael Patrick","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Christian Roos","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"923f8673-9ca6-4f97-99aa-4f579d638bde","name":"Pedersen, Christian Ross","name_org":"Pedersen, Christian Roos","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30020070"],"pub":{"issn":["00415782","13994174"],"issn_org":["00415782"],"issue":"29","journal":"Ugeskrift for Læger","paperid":"V10170787","status":"p","title":"Ugeskrift for Læger","title_org":"Ugeskrift for Laeger","volume":"180"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85128291206"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-303d2a92-45c6-4061-9fb0-fae9274cc04c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Svære komplikationer efter tab af galdesten under laparoskopisk kolecystektomi","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-422206","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Aims/hypothesis: Circulating metabolites have been shown to reflect metabolic changes during the development of type 2 diabetes. In this study we examined the association of metabolite levels and pairwise metabolite ratios with insulin responses after glucose, glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) and arginine stimulation. We then investigated if the identified metabolite ratios were associated with measures of OGTT-derived beta cell function and with prevalent and incident type 2 diabetes. Methods: We measured the levels of 188 metabolites in plasma samples from 130 healthy members of twin families (from the Netherlands Twin Register) at five time points during a modified 3 h hyperglycaemic clamp with glucose, GLP-1 and arginine stimulation. We validated our results in cohorts with OGTT data (n = 340) and epidemiological caseâ€“control studies of prevalent (n = 4925) and incident (n = 4277) diabetes. The data were analysed using regression models with adjustment for potential confounders. Results: There were dynamic changes in metabolite levels in response to the different secretagogues. Furthermore, several fasting pairwise metabolite ratios were associated with one or multiple clamp-derived measures of insulin secretion (all p <9.2 Ã— 10âˆ’7). These associations were significantly stronger compared with the individual metabolite components. One of the ratios, valine to phosphatidylcholine acyl-alkyl C32:2 (PC ae C32:2), in addition showed a directionally consistent positive association with OGTT-derived measures of insulin secretion and resistance (p â‰¤ 5.4 Ã— 10âˆ’3) and prevalent type 2 diabetes (ORVal_PC ae C32:2 2.64 [Î² 0.97 Â± 0.09], p = 1.0 Ã— 10âˆ’27). Furthermore, Val_PC ae C32:2 predicted incident diabetes independent of established risk factors in two epidemiological cohort studies (HRVal_PC ae C32:2 1.57 [Î² 0.45 Â± 0.06]; p = 1.3 Ã— 10âˆ’15), leading to modest improvements in the receiver operating characteristics when added to a model containing a set of established risk factors in both cohorts (increases from 0.780 to 0.801 and from 0.862 to 0.865 respectively, when added to the model containing traditional risk factors + glucose). Conclusions/interpretation: In this study we have shown that the Val_PC ae C32:2 metabolite ratio is associated with an increased risk of type 2 diabetes and measures of insulin secretion and resistance. The observed effects were stronger than that of the individual metabolites and independent of known risk factors.","author_count":44,"contributors":["Molnos, Sophie","Wahl, Simone","Haid, Mark","Eekhoff, E. Marelise W.","Pool, René","Floegel, Anna","Deelen, Joris","Much, Daniela","Prehn, Cornelia","Breier, Michaela","Draisma, Harmen H.","van Leeuwen, Nienke","Simonis-Bik, Annemarie M.C.","Jonsson, Anna Elisabet","Willemsen, Gonneke","Bernigau, Wolfgang","Wang-Sattler, Rui","Suhre, Karsten","Peters, Annette","Thorand, Barbara","Herder, Christian","Rathmann, Wolfgang","Roden, Michael","Gieger, Christian","Kramer, Mark H.H.","van Heemst, Diana","Pedersen, Helle Krogh","Gudmundsdottir, Valborg","Schulze, Matthias B.","Pischon, Tobias","de Geus, Eco J.C.","Boeing, Heiner","Boomsma, Dorret I.","Ziegler, Anette G.","Slagboom, P. Eline","Hummel, Sandra","Beekman, Marian","Grallert, Harald","Brunak, Søren","McCarthy, Mark I.","Gupta, Ramneek","Pearson, Ewan R.","Adamski, Jerzy","'T Hart, Leen M."],"cuid":["S02-099.49-038054"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["'T Hart, Leen M."],"country":["Germany","Netherlands","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","German Center for Diabetes Research","German Institute of Human Nutrition Potsdam-Rehbruecke","Helmholtz Zentrum München - German Research Center for Environmental Health","Leiden University Medical Center","Max Delbrück Center for Molecular Medicine in the Helmholtz Association","University of Dundee","University of Oxford","VU University Medical Centre","Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam"],"keyword":["Epidemiology","Insulin secretion","Journal Article","Metabolomics","Type 2 diabetes"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"metabolite ratios as potential biomarkers for type 000000000002 diabetes a direct study","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-017-4436-7","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.49-038054","identifier":[{"id":"85029711516","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28936587","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6448944","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["dtu-f5ff367d-1c61-49d7-aa91-52f502969e54","ku-0407d627-e8fc-466c-bb1b-b30f520d85b6"],"keyword":["Epidemiology","Insulin secretion","Journal Article","Metabolomics","Prediction of diabetes","Type 2 diabetes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-5689D785226CEA0DF495F9D00DA7461A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-017-4436-7","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/140541874/10.1007_2Fs00125_017_4436_7_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/222166557/Molnos2018_Article_MetaboliteRatiosAsPotentialBio.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"helmholtz zentrum münchen german research center for environmental health","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Helmholtz Zentrum München - German Research Cen