{"abstract":"PtxY and PtxGd exhibit exceptionally high activity for oxygen reduction, both in the polycrystalline form and the nanoparticulate form. In order to understand the origin of the enhanced activity of these alloys, we have investigated thin films of these alloys on bulk Pt(111) crystals, i.e. Y/Pt(111) and Gd/Pt(111). These surfaces exhibit a 4-fold improvement over Pt(111). We observe the formation of a thick Pt overlayer after the electrochemical measurements, both on Y/Pt(111) and Gd/Pt(111). Using surface sensitive X-ray diffraction we revealed that crystalline closely packed Pt overlayers were formed. The diffraction experiments showed that the strain and crystallinity of the overlayers are strongly dependent on the electrochemical treatment, and in general show lateral compression.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Pedersen, Anders Filsøe","Ulrikkeholm, Elisabeth Therese","Escribano, Maria Escudero","Johansson, Tobias Peter","Malacrida, Paolo","Pedersen, Christoffer Mølleskov","Hansen, Martin","Jensen, Kim Degn","Rossmeisl, Jan","Friebel, Daniel","Nilsson, Anders","Chorkendorff, Ib","Stephens, Ifan"],"cuid":["S02-099.07-033503"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory"],"keyword":["Diffraction","Electrocatalysis","Oxygen reduction","PT 111","Pt alloys"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"probing the nanoscale structure of the catalytically active overlayer on pt alloys with rare earths","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access