{"abstract":"Plasmonic black surfaces formed by two-dimensional arrays of ultra-sharp convex metal grooves, in which the incident radiation is converted into gap surface plasmon polaritons (GSPPs) and subsequently absorbed (via adiabatic nanofocusing), are fabricated and investigated experimentally for gold, nickel, and palladium, using scanning electron microscopy, optical microscopy, and reflection spectroscopy for their characterization. Absolute reflectivity spectra obtained for all fabricated arrays demonstrate very efficient and broadband absorption of unpolarized light exceeding the level of 95%, averaged over the investigated wavelength range of 400-985 nm. The highest averaged absorption level (∼97%) is achieved with 250-nm-period arrays in palladium that also has the highest melting temperature (∼1552°C), promising thereby potential applications for broadband absorption, e.g., within thermophotovoltaics. For one-dimensional arrays, GSPPs are excited only with the electric field polarized perpendicular to the groove orientation, resulting in 94-96% absorption of the appropriately polarized light for the arrays in nickel and palladium while featuring practically flat surface reflectivity spectra for the orthogonal polarization. The largest ratio (∼10.7) between averaged reflectivities for orthogonal polarizations is achieved with the groove arrays in palladium, pointing thereby towards applications as broadband and low-dispersion linear polarizers operating in reflection, e.g., within ultra-fast optics.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Beermann, Jonas","Eriksen, René L.","Stær, Tobias Holmgaard","Pedersen, Kjeld","Bozhevolnyi, Sergey"],"cuid":["S02-096.84-000317"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"plasmonic black metals via radiation absorption by two dimensional arrays of ultra sharp convex grooves","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/srep06904","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-096.84-000317","identifier":[{"id":"84923371544","type":"scopus"},{"id":"WOS:000344231200001","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC4219155","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"25365991","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-061a9459-61e3-43a4-b289-9a1e9d621bcd","sdu-f3b9b569-2956-4461-85a6-11184b943ce1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D18E7FD14220A812BA7C2803F4B799C4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/srep06904","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/144197936/srep06904.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Department of Technology and Innovation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (ENG)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Physics and Nanotechnology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"J.","key":"beermannpau","last":"Beermann","locid":"da4d107b-a5da-429d-ba3b-850b08b96322","name":"Beermann, Jonas","name_org":"Beermann, J.","orcid":"0000-0001-9809-2430","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"R. L.","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","locid":"b3e301d7-bbe7-448f-bb53-09968ed57b02","name":"Eriksen, René Lynge","name_org":"Eriksen, R. L.","orcid":"0000-0003-4405-5831","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tobias Holmgaard","key":"stærpau","last":"Stær","locid":"f9c73bfe-cf15-440a-9aca-290bb9e2f2a5","name":"Stær, Tobias Holmgaard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"T.","key":"holmgaardpau","last":"Holmgaard","name":"Holmgaard, T.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kjeld","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"ff5fc0ab-1fa1-47a2-916c-8b7556f2f938","name":"Pedersen, Kjeld","orcid":"0000-0002-6835-1566","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"S. I.","key":"bozhevolnyipau","last":"Bozhevolnyi","locid":"9df17db0-2e69-41ff-8a38-8738093f0385","name":"Bozhevolnyi, Sergey I.","name_org":"Bozhevolnyi, S. I.","orcid":"0000-0002-0393-4859","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25365991"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/srep06904","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"6904","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84923371544"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-061a9459-61e3-43a4-b289-9a1e9d621bcd","sdu-f3b9b569-2956-4461-85a6-11184b943ce1"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Plasmonic black metals via radiation absorption by two-dimensional arrays of ultra-sharp convex grooves","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.84-000317","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Mean daytime ambulatory blood pressure (BP) values are considered to be lower than conventional BP values, but data on this relation among younger individuals <50 years are scarce. Conventional and 24-hour ambulatory BP were measured in 9550 individuals not taking antihypertensive treatment from 13 population-based cohorts. We compared individual differences between daytime ambulatory and conventional BP according to 10-year age categories. Age-specific prevalences of white coat and masked hypertension were calculated. Among individuals aged 18 to 30, 30 to 40, and 40 to 50 years, mean daytime BP was significantly higher than the corresponding conventional BP (6.0, 5.2, and 4.7 mm Hg for systolic; 2.5, 2.7, and 1.7 mm Hg for diastolic BP; all P<0.0001). In individuals aged 60 to 70 and ≥70 years, conventional BP was significantly higher than daytime ambulatory BP (5.0 and 13.0 mm Hg for systolic; 2.0 and 4.2 mm Hg for diastolic BP; all P<0.0001).The prevalence of white coat hypertension exponentially increased from 2.2% to 19.5% from those aged 18 to 30 years to those aged ≥70 years, with little variation between men and women (8.0% versus 6.1%; P=0.0003). Masked hypertension was more prevalent among men (21.1% versus 11.4%; P<0.0001). The age-specific prevalences of masked hypertension were 18.2%, 27.3%, 27.8%, 20.1%, 13.6%, and 10.2% among men and 9.0%, 9.9%, 12.2%, 11.9%, 14.7%, and 12.1% among women. In conclusion, this large collaborative analysis showed that the relation between daytime ambulatory and conventional BP strongly varies by age. These findings may have implications for diagnosing hypertension and its subtypes in clinical practice.","author_count":33,"contributors":["Conen, David","Aeschbacher, Stefanie","Thijs, Lutgarde","Li, Yan","Boggia, Jose","Asayama, Kei","Hansen, Tine W.","Kikuya, Masahiro","Björklund-Bodegård, Kristina","Ohkubo, Takayoshi","Jeppesen, Jørgen","Gu, Yu-Mei","Torp-Pedersen, Christian","Dolan, Eamon","Kuznetsova, Tatiana","Stolarz-Skrzypek, Katarzyna","Tikhonoff, Valerie","Schoen, Tobias","Malyutina, Sofia","Casiglia, Edoardo","Nikitin, Yuri","Lind, Lars","Sandoya, Edgardo","Kawecka-Jaszcz, Kalina","Mena, Luis","Maestre, Gladys E.","Filipovsky, Jan","Imai, Yutaka","O’Brien, Eoin","Wang, Ji-Guang","Risch, Lorenz","Staessen, Jan A."],"cuid":["S02-099.25-011851"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Aging","Blood Pressure","Blood Pressure Monitoring Ambulatory","Circadian rhythm","Cohort Studies","Cross Sectional Studies","Female","Humans","Male","Masked Hypertension","Middle Aged","Office Visits","Prevalence","White coat hypertension","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"age specific differences between conventional and ambulatory daytime blood pressure values","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.114.03957","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.25-011851","identifier":[{"id":"WOS:000343593700027","type":"wos"},{"id":"84925804158","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25185130","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-f831e621-0c49-44fd-89ca-00a0c85263a0","ku-ef2f241b-9934-4aa2-8bc5-d3492a3f2556"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Aging","Blood Pressure","Blood Pressure Monitoring, Ambulatory","Circadian Rhythm","Cohort Studies","Cross-Sectional Studies","Female","Humans","Male","Masked Hypertension","Middle Aged","Office Visits","Prevalence","White Coat Hypertension","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-8E6AD96B22929E9195DBC7E7B64C9432"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"